<Project>

  <!-- Repo-wide build policy. Deliberately small — anything project-specific
       belongs in the project file. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- A committed packages.lock.json for every project.
         Direct PackageReferences are already exact-pinned in the csproj files;
         the lock file is what pins the TRANSITIVE graph. Without it, a
         transitive dependency can change underneath a release and the only
         evidence is a different SBOM after the fact. With it, the change
         arrives as a reviewable diff in a pull request.

         RestoreLockedMode is deliberately NOT set here, and that is not an
         oversight. A lock file records two package references the SDK injects
         rather than the project declaring them — Microsoft.DotNet.ILCompiler
         and Microsoft.NET.ILLink.Tasks — pinned to the versions bundled with
         whichever SDK ran the restore. This repository builds on several SDKs
         at once: AlmaLinux 9 ships 10.0.111, and the
         mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:10.0 images behind the .deb and the APKs
         ship 10.0.400. A lock file written by one of them fails NU1004 on
         another — a patch-level difference is enough. Turning locked mode on
         globally would break the packaging pipeline the same day.

         Locked mode is enforced instead in the two places where the SDK is
         fixed to an exact version and the guarantee is worth something: the
         `lockfiles` drift guard in CI, and the jobs that generate the SBOMs.
         Both pin the SDK with actions/setup-dotnet. See
         docs/VERIFYING-RELEASES.md for what this does and does not pin. -->
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
